---
code: mark-standalone-rc-consumer
when-fires: the new-grammar mark parser found a standalone mark-block (no command to bind) carrying an rc-consumer or `reads`. oracle/predict/mark_grammar.rs.
why: 28A:rul-continuation-attachment: a standalone block has no statement to measure/back, so a verdict/observe there is unbacked ⇒ the block drops to ⊤ (`inv-top-reject`).
edit-loop: edit a sentence in the transcript below, then run `mise run loom:compile -- mark-standalone-rc-consumer && mise run loom:promote -- mark-standalone-rc-consumer`; prove it stuck with `mise run test`, which is also what catches anything else the promotion moved. `mise run loom:vars -- --all mark-standalone-rc-consumer` lists this case's values; type {{name}} to insert or move one.
---
-- oracle.sh --
# dorc-lang/v0.2
: sm.a.B@x

-- replay --
$ dorc lint oracle.sh --no-tools
dorc lint findings (advisory; the machine format `--format=jsonl` is the stable surface):

oracle.sh:
  error[mark-standalone-rc-consumer]: This mark stands on no command, so the
  cell it names has nothing to back it: `asserts`, `refutes`, and `reads` must
  trail the command whose exit code and execution they read. A standalone line
  may carry only non-rc verbs such as `disturbs`, `lends`, or `safe-across`.
        [ oracle.sh:2:3
     2 | : sm.a.B@x
       |   \______/
  error[oracle-file-not-load-inert]: A `# dorc-lang/v0.2`-marked oracle file
  must be inert to load -- nothing but function definitions and static
  assignments at top level -- and this is the first item that does more. Move
  top-level work into a role function or into the book; an oracle file is loaded
  for its definitions, never run.
        [ oracle.sh:2:1
     2 | : sm.a.B@x
       | \________/

